<p>
A couple of years ago, <a href="https://irreal.org/blog/?p=12270">I wrote about</a> Chris Maiorana&rsquo;a post suggesting that writers adopt a one-line-per-sentence workflow. You can read <a href="https://chrismaiorana.com/one-sentence-per-line/">his post on the matter</a> to see his arguments on why. At the time, I wrote that I used that method for my two books but because it worked better with the [GT]roff typesetter rather than for any writer workflow reasons. Since moving to Org mode for my writing I&rsquo;ve given it up not least because it doesn&rsquo;t play well with WordPress where a lot of my writing ends up.
</p>
<p>
Maiorana is back with <a href="https://chrismaiorana.com/line-highlighting-in-emacs/">another post on the subject</a> that those who embrace the method may find useful. His idea is to use Emacs&rsquo; <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Cursor-Display.html#index-hl_002dline_002dmode">hl-line-mode</a> to highlight the current line. That brings some other benefits to the one-line-per-sentence method that Maiorana describes in his latest post.
</p>
<p>
I used to use <code>hl-line-mode</code>, although not in conjunction with one-line-per-sentence, but stopped because it interacted poorly with some other mode—probably <code>visual-line-mode</code> where it highlights whole paragraphs. Even when I was using it, I sometimes found it annoying despite it being useful for locating the current line. I&rsquo;m sure I&rsquo;m in the minority on that so you should definitely give it a try if you&rsquo;re not familiar with it. It&rsquo;s builtin so you can simply toggle it on for the current buffer with <kbd>Meta</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <code>hl-line-mode</code> to try it out.</p>
